Here's how to plot a curve in pgfplots where y-coordinates are read from a file and x-coordinates are automatically incremented:

## Method 1: Using `\addplot table` with coordinate index

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{pgfplots}
\pgfplotsset{compat=1.18}

\begin{document}
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}
\addplot table[
    x expr=\coordindex,  % Use line number as x-coordinate (starts from 0)
    y index=0           % Use first column as y-coordinate
] {data};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}
```

## Method 2: Starting x-coordinates from 1 instead of 0

```latex
\addplot table[
    x expr=\coordindex+1,  % Start x from 1 instead of 0
    y index=0
] {data};
```

## Method 3: If your data file has no column structure (just values)

```latex
\addplot table[
    x expr=\coordindex,
    y expr=\thisrowno{0}
] {data};
```

## Example data file format

Your `data` file should look like this:
```
2.5
3.1
4.7
2.8
5.2
1.9
```

## Complete working example

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{pgfplots}
\pgfplotsset{compat=1.18}

\begin{document}
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
    xlabel={Index},
    ylabel={Value},
    grid=major
]
\addplot[mark=*, blue] table[
    x expr=\coordindex+1,
    y index=0
] {data};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}
```

The key points:
- `\coordindex` gives you the automatic line number (0-indexed)
- `y index=0` reads the first (and only) column as y-values
- Add `+1` to `\coordindex` if you want x-coordinates to start from 1
- Make sure your data file is in the same directory as your LaTeX file

